6. THE WALK-INVARIANT BOTH-ENDS CLAIM — RE-VERIFIED MYSELF, NO REGRESSION
-------------------------------------------------------------------------
The coordinator set this as the FAIL-grade tripwire: if the nil case does not
break identically, it is an SY4 regression. I ran it myself rather than
accepting the manifest's quoted output.

First attempt, the ruling's own SINGLE-MONTH remainder fixture: the invariant
did NOT break at all —
  PROBE-WALK WITH flag (SY4 active):      last=-1777.8234 want=-1777.8234 holds=true
  PROBE-WALK nil map (master-native path): last=-1277.8234 want=-1277.8234 holds=true
So I could not reproduce the worker's quoted failure from that fixture, and
a non-reproducing probe proves nothing either way. I therefore constructed
the case that actually trips the stated precondition — a MULTI-MONTH range
with one refund-dominant month, so per-month |sum| cannot partition
range-level |sum| (Jan: -1000 grocery, +4000 credit, flagged -500;
Feb: -5000 rent, -300 HI):
  PROBE-MM WITH flag (SY4 active)            last=-5973.9220 want=   26.0780 holds=false (Living=2000.00)
  PROBE-MM nil map (master-native path)      last=-5473.9220 want= -473.9220 holds=false (Living=2500.00)
  PROBE-MM VERDICT: with-flag holds=false ; nil holds=false
      -> SAME outcome => pre-existing/master-native, attributable
The break occurs IDENTICALLY on the master-native path with no SY4
involvement. The tripwire is not tripped: this is NOT an SY4 regression, and
it falls under the same out-of-scope carve-out ruling SY-2026-08-30d already
made for the HI-abs quirk. Recorded as F2 for the backlog.
Sanity check on the same run: Living = 2000 with the flag vs 2500 with nil —
|(-1000+4000-5000)| vs |(-1000+4000-500-5000)| — the set exclusion is doing
exactly the right thing at range level in the very fixture that breaks the
unrelated walk precondition.

